.SH NAME
malloc, free, realloc, calloc \- main memory allocator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBchar *malloc\fI(size)
.br
\fBunsigned \fIsize;
.PP
\fBfree\fI(ptr)
.br
\fBchar \fI*ptr;
.PP
\fBchar *realloc\fI(ptr, size)
.br
\fBchar \fI*ptr;
.br
\fBunsigned \fIsize;
.PP
\fBchar *calloc\fI(nelem, elsize)
.br
\fBunsigned \fInelem, elsize;\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Malloc
and
.I free
provide a simple general-purpose memory allocation package.
.B Malloc
returns a pointer to a block of at least
.I size
bytes beginning on a word boundary.
.PP
The argument to
.I free
is a pointer to a block previously allocated by
.BR malloc ;
this space is made available for further allocation,
but its contents are left undisturbed.
.PP
Disorder results if the space assigned by
.B malloc
is overrun or if some random number is handed to
.BR free .
.PP
.B Malloc
allocates the first sufficiently large contiguous reach of
free space found in a circular search from the last 
block allocated or freed,
coalescing adjacent free blocks as it searches.
It calls
.BR sbrk (2)
to get more memory from the system when there is no
suitable space already free.
.PP
.B Realloc
changes the size of the block pointed to by
.I ptr
to
.I size
bytes and returns a pointer to the block that is possibly moved.
The contents is unchanged up to the lesser of the new and old sizes.
.PP
.B Realloc
also works if
.I ptr
points to a block freed since the last call of
.B malloc, realloc,
or
.BR calloc ;
thus sequences of
.B free, malloc,
and
.B realloc
exploit the search strategy of
.B malloc
to do storage compaction.
.PP
.B Calloc
allocates space for
an array of
.I nelem
elements of size
.I elsize.
The space is initialized to zeros.
.PP
Each of the allocation routines returns a pointer
to space suitably aligned (after possible pointer coercion)
for storage of any type of object.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Malloc, realloc,
and
.B calloc
return a null pointer (0) if there is no available memory
or if the arena has been detectably 
corrupted by storing outside the bounds
of a block.
Therefore the user should explicitly check return values
and abort his program if he receives a 
null pointer to avoid unexpected results.
.SH LIMITATIONS
When
.B realloc
returns 0, the block pointed to by
.I ptr
can be destroyed.
